WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.869
A payment request carries a currency that may be null.

2
00:00:05.869 --> 00:00:08.687
You write a small function that throws if it is.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.044
Call it, then use the currency on the next line.

4
00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:13.223
Does this compile?

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.249
No.

6
00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:19.899
The compiler still sees a String?, and refuses the call.

7
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:20.875
Here's why.

8
00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:28.135
The compiler narrows a type, a smart cast, only from checks it can see in the function it is compiling.

9
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:31.296
It never opens the body of a function you call.

10
00:00:31.296 --> 00:00:34.448
That body could change, or live in another module.

11
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.222
A contract puts that fact on the outside.

12
00:00:37.222 --> 00:00:42.147
It's the first statement of the function: returns implies currency is not null.

13
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.480
The compiler reads the contract, never the body.

14
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.412
After the call, currency is a String, and the line compiles.

15
00:00:49.412 --> 00:00:52.231
That's how require and requireNotNull work.

16
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:55.893
But nothing checks the contract against the body.

17
00:00:55.893 --> 00:00:58.636
The compiler trusts a contract unconditionally.

18
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:03.102
So write one that says: if I return true, this event is Captured.

19
00:01:03.102 --> 00:01:05.579
Then make the body always return true.

20
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.571
Call it on a Failed event.

21
00:01:07.571 --> 00:01:09.615
It compiles without a warning.

22
00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:14.588
Then the JVM checks the cast it was given, and throws ClassCastException.

23
00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:20.567
One level down, javap shows no trace of the contract in the bytecode.

24
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:25.254
It lives in Kotlin metadata, which only the Kotlin compiler reads.

25
00:01:25.254 --> 00:01:27.100
Java callers never see it.

26
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:33.403
Contracts are Kotlin only, and still Experimental in Kotlin 2.4, so you opt in.

27
00:01:33.403 --> 00:01:38.500
Java doesn't track null in its types, so the question never comes up there.

28
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.907
The rule: the compiler learns only from what it can see.

29
00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:47.978
A contract tells it what a function guarantees, and it believes you without checking.

30
00:01:47.978 --> 00:01:50.424
So keep the body as small as the promise.

31
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:51.407
Quick check.

32
00:01:51.407 --> 00:01:57.017
You change requireCurrency to log a warning instead of throwing, and keep the contract.

33
00:01:57.017 --> 00:01:58.952
Does the caller still compile?

34
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:01.217
Yes.

35
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:04.622
The compiler reads the contract, never the body.

36
00:02:04.622 --> 00:02:09.657
The promise is now a lie, and a null reaches the next line at run time.
